\use{document}

\title {A Tour of the texish Article Format}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This document exercises the article-style features of \texttt{document.texish}:
sectioning, figures with captions, lists, mathematics, monospaced code, coloured
text, clickable links, and the typographic niceties --- smart quotes, dashes, and
ellipses --- that come for free with the body font. It draws inspiration from a
plain \LaTeX{} article without trying to reproduce all of \LaTeX.
\end{abstract}

\section {Figures and images}

An image is placed with \texttt{includegraphics}, which accepts a width given
directly or as a fraction of the line width. Wrapping it in a figure adds an
automatically numbered caption.

\figure[h]{
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.35\linewidth]{frog.jpg}}
\vskip 6pt
\centerline{\caption{A frog, scaled to a third of the text width.}}
}

The frog above is a JPEG, decoded directly into the page. The same command reads
PNG images too, and a bare \texttt{scale} or an explicit height work as well.

\section {Mathematics}

Inline mathematics sits in the run of text, like the bound $0 \le x \le 1$ or the
sum $\sum_{i=1}^{n} i = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}$. A displayed equation is centred on its
own line:

$$ \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} $$

Roman words inside a formula use \texttt{text}, as in $V = \text{volume}$, and a
calligraphic letter comes from \texttt{mathcal}, as in the category $\mathcal{C}$
or the power set $\mathcal{P}(S)$. A square root draws its radical over the whole
radicand, whether a single letter like $\sqrt{\pi}$, a number like $\sqrt{2}$, or
an expression like $\sqrt{x + y}$.

\section {Lists}

Numbered and bulleted lists nest cleanly:

\begin{enumerate}
\item first point,
\item second point, long enough to wrap onto a second line so the continuation
aligns under the text rather than the number,
\item third point.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{itemize}
\item a bullet,
\item another bullet.
\end{itemize}

\section {Code, colour, and links}

Set a file name or a snippet in a monospaced face with \texttt{texttt}, e.g.
\texttt{document.texish} or \texttt{\\includegraphics\{frog.jpg\}}. Colour a word
with \textcolor{red}{textcolor}, or switch the pen for the rest of a group.

Links are real and clickable in the PDF. Visit the
\href{https://www.overleaf.com/learn}{help library} for tutorials, or write a
bare address such as \url{https://example.org} that links to itself.

